From 8783778e37eb91ebb985d95fd1ff105363cf3a3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 22:16:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/46] preprocessor: keep every token's physical line and
 column

Diagnostics and -g line tables take their positions from the preprocessed
text: line markers plus line counting, columns from the text itself. Five
constructs broke that, each measured against GCC 16.2's diagnostics:

- A backslash splice joined lines and nothing re-synchronized, so every
  multi-line #define shifted all later lines (until the next #include).
- A multi-line block comment collapsed to one space, the same way.
- A multi-line macro or function invocation was accumulated with its line
  breaks, which survived expansion, and then padded with blank lines as
  well: every multi-line call (f(1,\n 2)) shifted later lines by one per
  extra line.
- `__LINE__` in an accumulated invocation resolved against a flat resolver
  pinned to the invocation's LAST line: ADD(__LINE__,\n__LINE__) was 2*L2
  (GCC and Clang: L1+L2) and a multi-line assert() reported its last line.
- `#line` changed only __LINE__/__FILE__, never the source manager, so
  diagnostics in generated code (yacc, re2c, gforth) ignored it.

Fixes, in the same order of the pipeline:

- Splices on ordinary lines that provably cannot change tokens (outside
  literals and // comments, white space on one side, next line not a `#`)
  keep the physical line break; others are joined. A conservative lexical
  scan decides, and every misjudgement falls back to joining.
- Multi-line comments on ordinary lines become their line breaks plus
  spaces to the tail's column, as `gcc -E` does; inside a directive (or
  where a split would make a stray `#` a directive) they still collapse.
- Each pipeline iteration contributes one output line, so a line that
  starts more than one physical line after its predecessor is preceded by
  the missing blank lines (counted, inside an accumulation). Lines that
  belong to an invocation without text (directives, inactive lines) enter
  the buffer as empty lines.
- An outermost invocation spanning lines re-emits its consumed line breaks
  after its expansion, plus spaces to the column of what follows (line
  breaks surviving inside the expansion become spaces: its tokens belong to
  the expansion point), and the flush pads only what is missing.
- Accumulated text gets a per-line resolver; arguments of source-level
  invocations are prescanned unpinned at their own offsets, so an
  argument's __LINE__ is its own line and a nested invocation pins its body
  to its own name's line (GCC's rule; all probes match GCC 16.2).
- #line emits a line marker (GNU linemarker flags carried over), before the
  last flushed line when it appears inside an invocation; the include
  return marker uses the presumed line and file after a #line.

Also: the comment map activated mid-file no longer seeds the current line
with the file's first line, a newline inside a literal records the line's
real stripped offset, and an orphaned doc comment is back on its function.

Remaining inexactness is inherent to a text pipeline and matches `gcc -E`:
tokens after a mid-token splice (`+\<nl>+`) keep the joined line.

Tests: physical_line_tests.rs (16 end-to-end position tests: each marker
token names its expected line and column, from GCC 16.2's diagnostics) and
4 stripper/scanner unit tests; two mutants of the fix fail 5 of them. The
linear-time test's fixture now uses a mid-token splice and a directive
comment so that it still builds both maps.
