From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 11:40:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 16/46] i686: compare narrow values at their width; keep the
 memory and 32-bit forms where exact

new-A: a narrow BinOp result lives in a 32-bit register with unspecified
upper bits (`addl` of two U8 lanes leaves 260 where the value is 4).  The
fused compare-and-branch carried its own copy of the compare emitter that
hard-coded cmpl/testl and branched on those bits.  Both the value compare
and the fused branch now use emit_int_cmp_flags_staged, which compares at
the IR width (cmp{b,w,l}/test{b,w,l}).

Width-correct emission must not cost code where the 32-bit form was exact:

- peephole fold_narrow_load_imm_compare: a width-matched
  `cmp{b,w} $I,%low` / `test{b,w} %low,%low` after `mov{z,s}{b,w}l M,%R`
  becomes `cmp{b,w} $I|$0, M`.  This is exact for every flag (same width,
  same operands), so it needs no immediate window and no flags-reader gate,
  and sign-extending loads qualify.  The older 32-bit consumers are gated on
  zero extension.
- compute_narrow_images: values whose 32-bit image is a proven extension
  (narrow Load seeds; full-width Copy/Phi propagation; optimistic fixpoint).
  A byte compare in %esi/%edi/%ebp, which have no byte sub-register, then
  runs as `cmpl` when both images are the extension the predicate needs
  (zero: unsigned and Eq/Ne; sign: signed and Eq/Ne) instead of staging
  through %eax/%ecx.  wide_cmp_kind is checked exhaustively against the
  narrow semantics (all 8-bit patterns, 16-bit boundaries and a stride).
