From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:47:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/46] i686 inline asm: narrow outputs leave a fully defined
 value home

A `char` output promoted to an SSA value was stored with `movb` into its
4-byte home, but 32-bit readers read the home whole -- notably the
typeless CondBranch that `if (r)` simplifies to (`cmpl $0, home`), which
then tested three stale bytes.  Outputs into a promoted value's home now
store the IR type's extension (`movsbl/movzbl/movswl/movzwl` + `movl`, the
same choice as a narrow Load; `=@cc` stores its already zero-extended
register), matching x86-64.  Outputs into a C object (alloca, or through a
pointer) keep the exact-width store so neighbouring bytes survive.

Regression i686_asm_narrow_output_home.c: char/short/=@cc outputs with a
poisoned stack (3/6 wrong on the parent at -O1/-O2) plus adjacent
struct-field and array-element outputs that must not be clobbered.
