From ef0f76b10789379a3aba4dae5ec765852de75954 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 21:17:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/46] lowering: conversions start from the lowered value's
 width; scalar compound literals convert

get_expr_type reports the widened LP64 storage type (I64 for int-op-literal),
not the width of the bits lower_expr produces (I32). Twenty-one sites
lowered an expression and then converted it from get_expr_type, so a
negative int result was zero-extended: `double a[2] = { i, i + 1 }` with
i = -2 stored 4294967295. Affected: array/struct/designated/nested brace
initializers, braced scalars, vector initializers and compound literals,
vector-scalar splats (v + (i - 3), v += i - 3), __builtin_creal, scalar
returns from complex functions, _Float128 compound assignment, scalar->
vector casts, VLA bounds. All now use value_ir_type, the existing helper
that mirrors lower_arithmetic_binop for exactly this purpose. On i686 the
phantom 64-bit source also crashed the backend ("wide i686 cast reached the
scalar cast emitter").

A scalar compound literal `(double){ i - 3 }` stored the initializer's raw
bits without any conversion; it now converts as by initialization
(C11 6.5.2.5p4, 6.7.9p11), and an empty list is zero.

init_conversion_value_width.c checks 50 constructs at i = -7..7 against
values from plain declarations: upstream main 644f4145c fails 23 of them
(224 checks at -O0, plus the -m32 crash), GCC 16.2 and Clang 23.1.3 pass,
this commit passes at -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3 and -m32 -O0/-O2.
