From e7b160c095def25a91544c8a70a7954d7b4b5912 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 00:24:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 40/46] loop_idiom: widen the ctz idiom (AShr, narrow
 words/counters) and sink the count below the guard

The ffs/ctz recognizer refused arithmetic shifts, narrow words and narrow
counters, and placed the count in the guard block:

* AShr: after k shifts bit 0 is bit k of the original word whatever the
  fill bit, so `x >>= 1` on a signed word is the same loop.
* Narrow words (u8/i16): the header tests `Cast(x) & 1`; the polarity
  matcher now looks through an integer widening cast. The count is formed
  on the word widened to the counter type (sound: x != 0 and extension
  keeps the lowest set bit), so no separate zero-extension remains.
* Narrow counters: the step `(T)((int)r + 1)` sums in the promoted type and
  truncates once (`r0 + ctz mod 2^w`), matching the loop's wraparound.
* Placement: with a conditional preheader whose exit has no other
  predecessors and no phis, the count goes to the head of the exit block,
  so the zero path skips it and x is not live past the test (i686 no
  longer needs a callee-saved home for x across bsf). Debug spans of the
  receiving block stay index-parallel (the old preheader append left them
  misaligned under -g).

Tests: loop_idiom unit tests (sar, narrow counter truncation, sink and
phi-exit refusal); tests/regression/ffs_ctz_idiom_edges{,_m32} cover 13
shapes incl. negative ones (word masked/truncated after the guard, word
escaping the loop); output equals GCC 16.2 at -O0..-O3, x86-64 and -m32.
Oracle (6 ffs functions, -O2 static insns): lccc 49, GCC 16.2 69,
Clang 23.1 85, ICX 2026.2 85.
