From b4ec336ee72d67a50d1a841de475b0579142daf6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@arena.invalid>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 00:51:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 41/46] sema: enforce the lvalue constraints;
 __builtin_choose_expr lvalues store

lccc had no lvalue check for `=`, compound assignment, `++`/`--` or `&`
operands it could not lower as lvalues: `f() = 1`, `x + 1 = 2`, `(a, b) = 3`,
`sf().m = 5`, `f4f().x = 1` (ext-vector selection of an rvalue) all
compiled to a store that went nowhere. GCC 16.2 and Clang 23.1.3 reject
every one (C11 6.5.16p2, 6.5.2.4p1, 6.5.3.1p1, 6.5.3.2p1).

* `is_known_rvalue`: a deny-list of forms both oracles treat as rvalues;
  `.member`, vector lanes, ext-vector selections, __real__/__imag__ and the
  chosen arm of __builtin_choose_expr inherit their operand's category; a
  GNU statement expression its tail's (GCC), except under an ext-vector
  element (Clang, the only ext-vector oracle, treats it as an rvalue).
  Anything unmodelled is assumed an lvalue, so an omission cannot reject a
  valid program.
* Clang's spellings and precedence: duplicate lanes, then the ext-vector
  step, then "assignment to cast is illegal, lvalue casts are not
  supported" / "expression is not assignable"; `&` says "cannot take the
  address of an rvalue of type 'T'" before "address of vector element
  requested" (`&f()[0]` was reported as the latter).
* Reversed vector subscripts (`0[v]`, `1[f()]`) are rejected as by both
  oracles: commutativity is pointer + integer only. Vector-typed operands
  are trusted by the subscript check; vector lanes now infer their element
  type; ExtVector prints in Clang's canonical order.
* Miscompile: `__builtin_choose_expr(0, x, y) += 7` and
  `__builtin_choose_expr(1, s, t).m = 4` dropped the store and
  `&__builtin_choose_expr(...)` was rejected; lower_lvalue,
  get_struct_base_addr and get_array_base_addr now go through the chosen
  arm (output equals GCC/Clang). Its condition selects by any constant
  width (`is_nonzero`), not only I32/I64.

Upstream #866 verdicts: AGREE with diagnosing rvalue-lane stores and with
GCC's statement-expression category for GNU vectors; DISAGREE with pinning
`0[f()]` and the ext-vector `({ v; })[0] = x` as valid (both rejected by
Clang 23.1.3; GCC rejects the reversed form) and with "not assignable" for
`&f()[0]` (Clang: "cannot take the address of an rvalue"). The gate now
carries 19 (form, Clang first diagnostic) pairs, each verified against
Clang 23.1.3; section 2 keeps the write-through on a vector_size type.

Gates: check_sema_constraints.sh gains the lvalue constraints (16 forms,
GCC and Clang reject all 16) and a run-time check of the valid lvalue
forms; its stale 'function pointer' expectation is GCC's 'fp'. It was an
allowlisted orphan and is now wired (fast, ~1 s). check_subscript_
diagnostics.py gains the two reversed-vector cases (GCC-verified).
Oracle matrix (64 forms, first error vs Clang 23.1.3): 58 identical; the
rest are typedef names Clang shows (a CType has none) and GCC's spelling
of the subscript rule, which that check pins.
